<!-- New Publication -->
<div class="publication-item">
    <div class="publication-header">
        <h3>Encoder-Encoder Cross Attention Based Contextual  Sarcasm Detection (Under Review: Journal of Big  Data)</h3>
        <span class="publication-status">Under Review</span>
    </div>
    <p class="publication-authors">Md. Minhazul Islam, Md Tanbeer Jubaer, Md. Azmain Yakin Srizon, Saad Aloteibi, Mohammad Ali Moni</p>
    <p class="publication-description">This paper presents a contextual encoder–encoder cross-attention framework for sarcasm detection in social media conversations. The model jointly analyzes Reddit parent comments and replies to capture contextual dependencies and implicit sarcastic cues often missed by text-only approaches. It introduces sarcasm-adaptive ELECTRA pretraining using Replaced Token Detection on the SARC V1 dataset and fine-tunes the model across benchmark datasets. Achieving up to 83.72% accuracy, the proposed approach outperforms existing methods, while ablation studies demonstrate the importance of both cross-attention and domain-specific pretraining for robust sarcasm understanding.</p>
    
</div><!-- New Publication -->
<!-- New Publication -->
<div class="publication-item">
    <div class="publication-header">
        <h3>ICFNet: An Incongruence-Aware Cross-Modal  Unified Framework for YouTube Clickbait  Detection with Multimodal LLM Comparison.  (Under Review: Information Processing And  Management) </h3>
        <span class="publication-status">Under Review</span>
    </div>
    <p class="publication-authors">Md. Minhazul Islam, Md. Masum Mia, Amith Khandakar, Mohamed Arselene Ayari, Md. Tajim An Noor, Muhammad Salman Khan, Hamed Noori</p>
    <p class="publication-description">This paper introduces ICFNet, an efficient multimodal framework for YouTube clickbait detection by identifying inconsistencies between video titles and thumbnails. The model combines RoBERTa and ConvNeXtV2 representations through bidirectional cross-attention and an incongruence-aware FiLM gated classifier. Evaluated on the newly introduced YTClickbait21K dataset with 21,238 videos, ICFNet achieves 92.04% accuracy and 97.51% AUC while training only 3.02M parameters using frozen backbones. Extensive experiments, cross-dataset evaluation, retrieval-augmented LLM comparison, and explainability analysis demonstrate its effectiveness, efficiency, and interpretability for multimodal misinformation detection.</p>
    
</div><!-- New Publication -->
<div class="publication-item">
    <div class="publication-header">
        <h3>YTClickbait21K: Human-Annotated MultimodalDataset for YouTube Clickbait Detection AcrossDiverse Channels and Content Categories</h3>
        <span class="publication-status">Under Review</span>
    </div>
    <p class="publication-authors">Md. Minhazul Islam, Md. Tanbeer Jubaer, Amith Khandakar, Shovon Sarker, Sumaiya Rahman, Md. Masum Mia, Mohamed Arselene Ayari, Hamed Noori</p>
    <p class="publication-description">YTClickbait21K: Human-Annotated Multimodal Dataset for YouTube Clickbait Detection Across Diverse Channels and Content Categories introduces a large-scale, human-annotated multimodal dataset for studying clickbait detection in online video platforms. The dataset contains 21,238 YouTube videos collected from 40 channels across 29 countries, covering diverse categories such as news, entertainment, education, and gaming. It provides aligned multimodal information, including video titles, thumbnails, metadata, and annotations, enabling research on multimodal learning for detecting misleading content. The dataset addresses limitations of existing clickbait benchmarks and supports the development of more reliable AI-based content understanding systems.</p>
    <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14780" target="_blank" class="publication-link">View Publication →</a>
</div><!-- New Publication -->
<!-- New Publication -->

</div><!-- New Publication -->
<div class="publication-item">
    <div class="publication-header">
        <h3>Multilingual Hate Speech and Slang Detection: Evaluating Hybrid Transformers Against Zero-Shot and Few-Shot LLMs Struggling with Short and Low-Context Texts</h3>
        <span class="publication-status">Accepted - ICCIT 2025</span>
    </div>
    <p class="publication-authors">Md Minhazul Islam; Most Anamika Akter Zedni; Azmain Yakin Srizon; Md Tanbeer Jubaer; Md Sanaul Haque Shanto</p>
    <p class="publication-description">This paper investigates multilingual hate speech and slang detection in short, low-context social media texts, comparing hybrid transformer-based models with zero-shot and few-shot large language models (LLMs). The study evaluates the challenges faced by LLMs in understanding implicit, slang-driven, and culturally dependent toxic expressions. Through extensive experiments across multilingual datasets, it demonstrates the effectiveness of task-specific hybrid transformer architectures for robust classification, while highlighting limitations of general-purpose LLMs under limited context conditions. The findings provide insights into developing more reliable and efficient multilingual content moderation systems.</p>
    <a href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/11491201" target="_blank" class="publication-link">View Publication →</a>
</div>
